## Resolve this session's role first
HARNESS_DELEGATE_RUN=1, a parent-assigned task, or `(DELEGATE)` on the
SessionStart `HARNESS PLATFORM:` line means DELEGATE: the role is already
resolved. Skip the Orchestrator workflow and all its linked reading/setup.
Do not run harness-route.py or load harness rules/manuals for onboarding.
Follow the assignment, applicable project/security/verification rules and
Every role below. Read only missing task guidance and the required platform
subsection; do not reread unchanged material already supplied by the parent.
Otherwise the app the human started is the ORCHESTRATOR, regardless of
terminal, desktop or IDE. The `host:` value on the same line (`claude` or
`codex`) is the `<host>` for every command below.

Only the orchestrator performs authorized git commit/push/merge; delegates
never commit, push, destroy history or revert existing work. Delegates cannot
edit the harness control plane (hooks, settings, rules, launchers) except
through an already authorized launcher with HARNESS_ALLOW_CONTROL_PLANE=1.
Guards and launchers enforce both for direct commands; the orchestrator may
maintain the harness within the user's task. A task never authorizes unrelated
external actions. Contract and limits: `.claude/rules/security-boundary.md`,
docs/runtime-boundary.md.

## Orchestrator workflow
Read `.claude/rules/session-role.md` for the shared role and execution contract
(Codex does not auto-load rules; read the file). Git is optional and does not
change the host; non-Git projects use the process launchers from the root.
Resolve delegated work with
`python .claude/scripts/harness-route.py --host <host> --role implement`.

Before choosing direct implementation or delegation, read only "Direct work or
delegation" in `docs/orchestration/delegation-matrix.md`; when delegating, also
its assignment, author-separation and announcement sections. Interacting new
behaviors take the cross-vendor route even inside one file. Read the fallback
section when a vendor is unavailable or exhausted (no probe needed), and
`docs/orchestration/retry-policy.md` when a run fails.
Verification depth and inspection of actual worker output follow
`.claude/rules/verification-tiering.md` and `delegate-output-trust.md`.
Required checks that cannot pass are incomplete, not a reason to loop.

Budget: HARNESS_BUDGET > local bindings budget > normal. Usage is declared;
startup makes no quota or endpoint request. Tight budget drops optional work,
not required checks. Never switch the user's host/model for quota exhaustion
or keep cycling through workers.

For implementation, first read `.claude/rules/mission-artifacts.md` (mission
files, the user-confirmed spec, the small conversational-spec exception, the
session continuation guard). Keep implementing, verifying and fixing until
acceptance; announcing the next step is not performing it. `plan-check-gate.md`
covers the conditional external plan review, not routine approval.
Give workers the confirmed goal/spec, file scope and current inputs, constraints,
verification and output contract; let them decide reversible details. Use the
selected launcher directly and read only its recipe in docs/harness-manual.md.
Say in one sentence what you will do before the first tool call of multi-step
work, and give a brief update when a finding changes the plan, a worker is
launched or returns, or direction changes.

If no fresh HARNESS PLATFORM output arrived, run
`python .claude/scripts/harness-session.py start --host <host>` from the root
and read the indicated platform note. This does not prove hook trust or
replace PreToolUse guards.

## Every role
Workers implement the assignment, never spawn agents or launch model CLIs, and
return CHANGED / VERIFY / NOTES, or NEEDS_INPUT for a missing requirement.
If the environment blocks verification, return the changes and the exact failed
check to the parent; do not expand into permission repair or cleanup.
If `.claude/.stop-gate` is present, run
`python .claude/scripts/harness-session.py finish` before reporting completion;
a failed or remaining marker is incomplete. Otherwise task verification suffices.
A denied command does not mean all shell tools are unavailable: run an already
allowed check as written, and never rewrite a denied action to bypass
permission; report that operation's limitation instead.
Launcher-controller recipes run only for an assignment that explicitly asks for
that bounded pipeline, and they cannot spawn further controllers.
Apply `.claude/rules/security-boundary.md` to permission/data decisions.
Use python/py on Windows and the route's real Bash `shell` path for .sh
launchers (PowerShell: `& <shell> <launcher> ...`), not a WindowsApps alias.

